                                                                  2625 1451
 This evaluation has been developed in support of the             2625 1451
 international CIELO project.                                     2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
 The evaluation, file assembly and criticality validation         2625 1451
 were performed at BNL and IAEA by                                2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
 G.P.A. Nobre, D.A. Brown, M. Herman, R. Arcilla, R. Capote,      2625 1451
 A. Trkov                                                         2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
 Primary reference: Nucl. Data Sheets 148, 214 (2018)             2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
 The evaluation was performed up to 150 MeV for 7 subsequent      2625 1451
 neutron emissions, 3 proton emissions and a single alpha-        2625 1451
 particle, deuteron, triton, and 3He for a total of 146           2625 1451
 residual nuclei. Only 5 of these were treated as exclusive,      2625 1451
 i.e., had an explicit reaction assigned to them along with the   2625 1451
 corresponding exclusive spectra for all particles and gammas.    2625 1451
 The exclusive reactions were (n,g), (n,elas), (n,inel), (n,      2625 1451
 2n), (n,p), and (n, 2p). For all the remaining we provide        2625 1451
 cross sections for residua production which represent a sum of   2625 1451
 contributions from all possible reactions populating a given     2625 1451
 residue. Their respective spectra are lumped together            2625 1451
 providing only seven cumulative spectra (for gammas, neutrons,   2625 1451
 protons, alphas, deuterons, tritons, and 3He) for all the        2625 1451
 inclusive channels.                                              2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
 Development of the evaluation is traced in the                   2625 1451
 CIELO-iron project on the NNDC GForge server:                    2625 1451
 https://ndclx4.bnl.gov/gf/project/cielo_iron                     2625 1451
 All elements of the evaluation, such as experimental datasets,   2625 1451
 EMPIRE inputs and outputs, intermediate ENDF files and plots     2625 1451
 can be followed there in a chronological order.                  2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
 EXPERIMENTAL DATBASE                                             2625 1451
 --------------------                                             2625 1451
 The experimental database used in the evaluation is summarized   2625 1451
 in Table IX of the primary reference (NDS 148,241(2018)). In     2625 1451
 doing this selection we favored: (i) broad energy range covered  2625 1451
 by the experiment, (ii) high energy resolution, and (iii) high   2625 1451
 (but reasonable) accuracy of the experiment. Thus the            2625 1451
 measurements providing possibly wide and reliable picture were   2625 1451
 given precedence over single energy-point experiments,           2625 1451
 especially within the energy range where strong fluctuations     2625 1451
 were observed. On the other hand, many single- or a few-point    2625 1451
 measurements adopted when no extended datasets were              2625 1451
 available. Some of the measured datasets were renormalized to    2625 1451
 account for changes in the reference cross sections since they   2625 1451
 were used in the analysis of the experiments. The                2625 1451
 renormalizations built into the EXFOR retrieval system were      2625 1451
 adopted. In case of the (n,p) and (n,2n) reactions we took       2625 1451
 advantage of the existing high quality evaluations available     2625 1451
 from the IRDFF library (INDC(NDS)-0616, IAEA, Vienna (2012))     2625 1451
 and used these data instead of the actual measurements.          2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
 RESOLVED RESONANCE RANGE                                         2625 1451
 ------------------------                                         2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
 MF=2                                                             2625 1451
  MT=151 Resolved resonances                                      2625 1451
    The resonance region evaluation for 54Fe was initially        2625 1451
    evaluated at IRSN, up to 1.036 MeV, by using SAMMY to fit     2625 1451
    Pandey et al. [1] and Cornelis et al. [2] data. In both cases 2625 1451
    a time of flight correction was applied, accounting for energy2625 1451
    shifts between fits and unmodified data retrieved from EXFOR. 2625 1451
    The evaluation uses the Reich-Moore approximation and the     2625 1451
    LRF=7 format. The initial J and parity assignments of         2625 1451
    resonances were taken from the Atlas of Neutron Resonances.   2625 1451
    As neither Cornelis et al. nor Pandey et al. resolved all     2625 1451
    resonances, the IRSN fits were supplemented with 64 resonances2625 1451
    compiled in the newest edition of the Atlas, measured mostly  2625 1451
    by Giubrone et al. [3]. Even with the addition of these p-    2625 1451
    and d-wave resonances, the average capture cross section      2625 1451
    was substantially lower than the measured average capture     2625 1451
    data of Allen et al. (1976) [4]. We computed the capture      2625 1451
    background from 100 keV-1 MeV by subtracting the group-wise   2625 1451
    cross section determined from the resonance parameters from   2625 1451
    the Allen et al. data. This not only brought the average      2625 1451
    cross section into agreement with Allen et al. [4] data, but  2625 1451
    also with the new Wallner data [5]. To ensure a proper match  2625 1451
    of the fast region capture onto the average resonance region  2625 1451
    capture cross section, we extended our EMPIRE calculations    2625 1451
    to 10 eV and tuned our calculation to match the average data  2625 1451
    of Allen et al. (1976)  [4].                                  2625 1451
    The evaluation compares favorably with Atlas values for both  2625 1451
    the thermal cross sections and resonance integrals, but it    2625 1451
    does not match the KADoNiS-0.3 recommended value for MACS(30  2625 1451
    keV)  [6]. This is because the KADoNiS recommendation was     2625 1451
    based on a preliminary version of the Wallner data.           2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
     Calculated 2200-m/s cross sections and res. integrals        2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
             thermal   res. integ.    MACS (30 keV)               2625 1451
                                                                  2625 1451
    total    4.417 b   125.96 b                                   2625 1451
    elastic  2.162 b   121.60 b                                   2625 1451
    capture  2.25  b     1.21 b       27.13 mb                    2625 1451
